        ":question/id": "f8ebc497-48a2-5b22-a14f-221db06dd5d2",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-44404",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x+5y=10?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+5y=10,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5y & =10 \\\\ 5y & =-x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=5x-4$ has a slope of $5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot 5=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=5x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line."
      },
