        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-44514",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $12x-4y=5?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $12x-4y=5,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-4y & =5 \\\\ -4y & =-12x+5 \\\\ y & =3x-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $3.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+3$ has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+3$ is perpendicular to the given line."
      },
