        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-23378",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $5x-y=7?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $5x-y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-y & =7 \\\\ -y & =-5x+7 \\\\ y & =5x-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $5.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{5}x-6$ has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{1}{5}x-6$ is perpendicular to the given line."
