        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-23621",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x+3y=6?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+3y=6,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =6 \\\\ 3y & =-x+6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=3x$ has a slope of $3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=3x$ is perpendicular to the given line."
